From 0725a2c279d3421ece6aa9eebb5c8993c1822247 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darcy Phipps <dphipps@qnx.com>
Date: Mon, 8 Dec 2025 16:25:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Fix bug when detecting on-target compiler for tests

The test code, especially the f2py test code, wants to find an on-target
compiler (meson) to help execute the tests. There is an explicit check
to make sure such a compiler exists before running the test. If it
doesn't exist, then the test is skipped.

The check had a bug where it would treat a subprocess.CalledProcessError
as an indication that the compiler is absent. However this is only
thrown when both check==True and the subprocess returns a non-zero
return code. Since check is explicitly set to False, it will never get
thrown.

Instead, catch a FileNotFoundError. That appears to be what is thrown
when the compiler can't be found.
---
 numpy/f2py/tests/util.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/numpy/f2py/tests/util.py b/numpy/f2py/tests/util.py
index 35e5d3bd8ac0..ea98ba3c08c0 100644
--- a/numpy/f2py/tests/util.py
+++ b/numpy/f2py/tests/util.py
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@ def check_language(lang, code_snippet=None):
                 cwd=tmpdir,
                 capture_output=True,
             )
-        except subprocess.CalledProcessError:
+        except FileNotFoundError:
             pytest.skip("meson not present, skipping compiler dependent test", allow_module_level=True)
         return runmeson.returncode == 0
     finally:

From f6c94adf2a34c33e95f31f39cf84f15690de86a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
